    "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1610375461246-83df859d849d?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1200&q=80",
    "excerpt": "A practical, ground-level guide to buying gold in Pakistan's Sarafa Bazaars. Learn how to convert between tolas and grams, avoid hidden making charges, protect your resale value, and track real-time bullion rates.",
    "content": "Step into any busy Sarafa Bazaar in Lahore, Karachi, or Rawalpindi, and you are immediately surrounded by the rapid chatter of tolas, mashas, ratis, pure 24K bars, and complex making charge calculations. \n\nFor generations, families across Pakistan have trusted gold as the ultimate shield against local currency devaluation and inflation. Yet, despite buying gold all our lives, many of us still walk out of jewelry shops paying 5% to 15% above the actual market value—or taking a massive hit when we try to sell those ornaments back a few years later.\n\nGetting the best value out of your gold isn't just for professional traders. Whether you are building an emergency nest egg or shopping for a wedding, here is the straightforward, no-nonsense breakdown you need before putting your money on the counter.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Tolas vs. Grams: Understanding Local Weights\n\nWhile international financial markets quote gold in Troy Ounces ($USD/oz), Pakistan’s local Sarafa market stubbornly sticks to the traditional weight system alongside metric grams.\n\n* **1 Tola** = exactly 11.664 Grams (though most retail jewelers conveniently round it down to 11.66g on their scales).\n* **1 Tola** = 12 Mashas\n* **1 Masha** = 8 Ratis\n\nWhen checking live price trackers like FGC Spot, you will usually see rates displayed per 10 grams and per tola. Whenever you place jewelry on a shop counter, always ensure their digital weighing scale is clearly showing grams down to two decimal places (like 11.66g) rather than accepting vague verbal estimates.\n\n---\n\n### 2. 24 Karat vs. 22 Karat: Where Should Your Money Go?\n\nGold purity is measured on a 24-part scale, which changes everything depending on your purpose:\n\n* **24 Karat (99.9% Pure):** This is raw, unadulterated bullion—minted biscuits and certified bars from trusted refineries like ARY or local certified brands. Because pure 24K gold is exceptionally soft and malleable, it bends easily under pressure and is practically never used to craft wearable jewelry.\n* **22 Karat (91.6% Pure):** The industry standard for everyday jewelry. Jewelers blend roughly 2 parts of strengthening alloys (like copper, silver, or zinc) with 22 parts pure gold so the ornament can hold its shape, secure stones, and withstand daily wear.\n* **21K and 18K:** Commonly found in delicate imported Italian chains or fine stone-studded rings.\n\n**The Golden Rule of Gold:** If your goal is purely investment, wealth preservation, or hedging against inflation, **never buy wearable jewelry**. You will lose money on the craftsmanship the second you walk out. Stick strictly to certified **24K cast bars or minted coins**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Decoding the \"Katt\" and Resale Deductions\n\nThe most painful part of buying gold usually comes years later when you try to liquidate old jewelry for cash. Jewelers apply deductions based on two main things:\n\n1. **Making Charges (Ujrat / Mazdoori):** When purchasing jewelry, you pay anywhere from 800 PKR to over 2,500 PKR per gram just for the artisan's labor. The moment you leave the store, that labor value drops to zero. When selling it back, nobody pays you for the making charges.\n\n2. **The \"Katt\" or Purity Deduction:** Even on pure 22K pieces, many traditional shops deduct an extra 1% to 2% from the weight, claiming it covers soldering waste (tanka) used to join links together.\n\n*A Quick Tip on Stones:* If your jewelry features heavy artificial stones, pearls, or glass beads, make sure the jeweler weighs the stones **separately** and deducts their weight before calculating the gold price. Otherwise, you end up paying gold rates for glass, only to lose that weight entirely during resale.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Your Pre-Purchase Checklist\n\nBefore handing over cash or trading in old ornaments at the bazaar, run through this quick checklist:\n\n* **Check the Live Sarafa Rate First:** Never walk into a shop blind. Check the live benchmark rates on FGC Spot. Local Sarafa associations update rates daily based on international spot market swings and the USD/PKR interbank rate.\n* **Get an Itemized Receipt:** Your purchase slip must explicitly state the gross weight, net gold weight (minus stones), karat purity, itemized making charges per gram, and the shop owner's signature or stamp.\n* **Look for the Hallmark Stamp:** Quality jewelry typically bears a tiny laser-engraved hallmark stamp (such as \"916\" for 22K or \"750\" for 18K) tucked away on an inner ring or clasp.\n\n---\n\n### Final Thoughts\n\nGold remains one of the most dependable pillars of financial security during volatile economic times. However, navigating retail markups, karat grades, and hidden deductions requires a bit of street smarts. Treat buying gold with the same care as buying property—verify your numbers, cross-check live market rates, and always read the fine print.\n",
